#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Classify git worktrees across many repos as dead / orphan / live / cannot-tell.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
A 14-day audit counted 593 worktrees across these repos and reported 217 dead
and 245 orphans. That classification is NOT usable as a delete list, for a
reason that has nothing to do with the count: the containment checker that
produced it called a `git diff --pathspec-from-file` that does not exist, exited
129, and therefore scored EVERY branch as unmerged. It was rebuilt and spot-
checked 7/7 against PR ground truth afterwards — but 7 confirmations is thin
support for 245 irreversible deletions, and "unmerged, no PR" is also the exact
profile of work in progress.

So this tool re-derives the classification from scratch, records the EVIDENCE
each verdict rests on, and refuses to guess.

🔴 THE TRAP THIS TOOL IS BUILT AROUND: A SQUASH MERGE IS NOT AN ANCESTOR
------------------------------------------------------------------------
`git merge-base --is-ancestor <branch> <default>` returns FALSE for every
squash-merged branch, forever — the squash is a new commit with different
parents, so the branch's own commits genuinely are not in the default branch's
history. A classifier that asks only that question reports merged work as
orphaned, which is the failure mode that gets real work deleted.

"Did this land" is therefore answered by FOUR independent signals, and the row
records which ones fired:

  ancestor          `git merge-base --is-ancestor` — definitive when true,
                    worthless when false (see above).
  pr-merged         `gh pr list --head <branch> --state all` reports MERGED.
                    Definitive, and the only signal that survives a squash the
                    default branch has since built on top of.
  content-identical every path the branch changed since its merge-base has the
                    SAME content in the default branch. This is the signal that
                    catches a squash merge with no git-level ancestry.
  patch-equivalent  `git cherry <default> <head>` finds an equivalent commit
                    upstream for every commit on the branch. Catches rebases
                    and cherry-picks.

🔴 `content-identical` HAS A KNOWN FALSE NEGATIVE, stated rather than implied:
if the branch squash-merged and the default branch has since CHANGED one of
those paths again, the contents no longer match and this signal goes quiet. That
is why `pr-merged` exists, and why a row with no signal at all and no
authoritative PR answer is `cannot-tell`, never `dead`.

🔴 AND A GUARD AGAINST THE MIRROR-IMAGE BUG: a comparison against an ABSENT
operand reports SAME, not MISSING. If the branch changed nothing since its
merge-base the changed-path set is EMPTY, and a pathspec-less `git diff` would
then compare the WHOLE TREES and answer a question nobody asked. That case is
short-circuited explicitly (`no-content-change`) and never reaches the diff.

THE VERDICTS
------------
  live         the main checkout, or locked, or DIRTY (any uncommitted or
               untracked file), or carrying an OPEN pull request.
  dead         clean, and at least one landing signal fired.
  orphan       clean, no landing signal, AND an authoritative PR answer says
               there is no open PR. Informational — NEVER removable.
  cannot-tell  everything else, loudly: gh unavailable or unauthenticated, a
               truncated PR index, a detached HEAD with no branch to look up, an
               unresolvable default branch, a git command that failed, a
               worktree whose path is gone. It is never silently folded into
               `dead`.

🔴 PATH EXCLUSION — AGENT WORKTREES ARE SPARED BY DEFAULT
---------------------------------------------------------
Repo-level scoping (`--repo` / `--repos-from` / `--scan-root`) used to be the
ONLY filter, and that is not fine-grained enough to be safe. Measured on this
machine — the numbers below are one scan, dated, and reproducible with the exact
command in `scripts/README.md`; do not restate them as a general truth about
the box:

    2026-08-27, `worktree-prune --scan-root /home/zach/workspace --scan-depth 2`
    860 worktrees / 129 repos -> 236 dead, 44 orphan, 528 live, 52 cannot-tell,
    every one of the 236 dead rows removable with the exclusion off.
    137 of those 236 sit under a `.claude/worktrees/` directory belonging to
    OTHER LIVE Claude sessions, so the default leaves 99 removable.

    🔴 MEASURED DRIFT, so a re-run that disagrees is not a bug. Two runs the
    same day returned `236 dead / 137 agent-dead / 99 removable` IDENTICALLY,
    while `orphan`/`cannot-tell` moved 44/52 -> 43/53 — one row's PR state or
    dirtiness changed between them.

    🔴 THAT IS n=2, AND IT IS NOT A STABILITY GUARANTEE — an earlier revision of
    this comment read "the numbers this design rests on are the stable ones",
    which the code cannot promise and which contradicts `--confirm` three
    sections below. `dead` and `removable` consume EXACTLY the inputs that moved
    in that pair: a worktree going clean flips `live -> dead -> removable`, and a
    PR merging flips `orphan -> dead`. They held still across two runs minutes
    apart; they are not pinned. `--confirm N` refusing because N moved is the
    tool working — the scope changed under you — not a tool bug, and that is the
    whole reason N is re-derived from a fresh scan instead of remembered.

🔴 EARLIER NUMBERS IN THIS FILE'S HISTORY ARE RETIRED, NOT RESTATED. A previous
revision cited "870 worktrees / 128 repos -> 250 dead … 106 removable" beside a
"131 agent worktrees" figure, and those three cannot all be true: 250 - 131 is
119, not 106. Nothing on this box could be re-run to reconcile them, so they are
gone rather than repeated. The block above is one run, with its date and its
exact scope, and nothing here claims to be a standing fact about the machine.

`dead` is a correct verdict about the BRANCH and a catastrophic instruction
about the DIRECTORY — removing one destroys a working directory that is in use
right now. Dodging them by dropping whole repos also drops the ordinary dead
rows those repos hold, because a repo like `civit/civitai` carries dozens of
each.

🔴 SO THE DEFAULT IS TO SPARE THEM. `AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB` is applied to every
run unless `--include-agent-worktrees` is passed. The dangerous action is the
one the operator has to type, not the one they get by forgetting a flag: on the
scan above the default spares all 137 and leaves 99 legitimately-dead worktrees
removable. (There is no `--skip-agent-worktrees`; it was the opt-IN spelling of
this and is gone rather than left as a no-op alias, because a flag that does
nothing is worse than one that does not exist.)

`--exclude-path GLOB` (repeatable) adds operator-typed filters at the row
level:

  * An excluded worktree is STILL scanned, classified and reported. It does not
    vanish from the output — silent truncation reads as "we covered everything",
    which is the failure this tool exists to not have. It is reported as
    `dead (excluded)` (or `live (excluded)`, …) and counted in its own column.
  * An excluded row is NEVER removable, whatever its verdict, and is NOT counted
    toward `--confirm N`. `--confirm N` keeps meaning "the exact number that will
    actually be removed".
  * 🔴 A TYPED GLOB THAT MATCHES NOTHING IS BYTE-IDENTICAL TO NO GLOB AT ALL.
    That is the worst failure this flag can have: the operator mistypes
    `*/.claude/wortrees/*`, reads "99 row(s) would be removed", passes
    `--confirm 99` — and `--confirm` provides ZERO independent protection,
    because N is derived from the same wrong count. So the report ALWAYS echoes
    the effective glob list with a PER-GLOB match count (per-glob, because a
    total is non-zero as soon as one of two globs works), names any glob that
    matched zero rows, and `--execute` REFUSES while a TYPED glob is in that
    state.

    🔴 TYPED, not "any". The refusal exists because a HUMAN can mistype a glob.
    `AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB` is a hardcoded constant, spelled once, pinned by a
    test — it cannot be mistyped, so it carries none of the hazard and is never
    a refusal candidate. Scoping this wrong made the safety flag block ITSELF:
    on any repo with no `.claude/worktrees` directory, `--skip-agent-worktrees
    --execute` refused with a remedy ("fix the glob, or drop it") that could
    only be satisfied by DROPPING THE SAFETY FLAG. That is the permanently-red
    gate, and the click-through it trained was the catastrophic action itself.

  * 🔴 `--allow-unmatched-globs` is the escape hatch for the OTHER half of the
    empty result. A zero match cannot distinguish "mistyped" from "correct but
    out of THIS scan's scope" — a fleet-wide exclude list applied to a one-repo
    scan is entirely correct and matches nothing. Passed, a zero-matching typed
    glob WARNS loudly (still named, still per-glob) instead of refusing. It is
    echoed prominently in the report whenever it is in force: an override that
    is not visible in the output is a hardcode wearing a costume.

  * 🔴 A scan that produced ZERO ROWS never refuses. With no rows, no glob could
    have matched anything, so the zero says nothing about the glob — and the
    run removes nothing regardless. It used to refuse, which broke the
    previously-working `--execute --confirm 0` no-op.
  * 🔴 GLOB SEMANTICS, ALL FOUR PINNED BY TESTS. Matching is
    `fnmatch.fnmatchcase` against the ABSOLUTE path git reports:
      - **`*` CROSSES `/`** — not shell/`pathlib` globbing. `*/civitai/*`
        matches every path with a `civitai` component.
      - **SUBTREE**: a glob matching any ANCESTOR excludes the worktree too, so
        `--exclude-path <dir>` covers the directory's CONTENTS. Chosen over
        auto-appending `/*` (which would stop the glob matching the directory
        itself, and is meaningless for a glob already ending in `*`) and over a
        second `--exclude-tree` flag (a second rule is a second thing to drift).
        Before this, naming a repo matched the repo's own row and nothing else,
        printing a reassuring "1 row(s) matched … 0 of them are dead" while both
        dead children stayed removable.
      - **surrounding whitespace and a trailing `/` are stripped**, in that
        order and identically at both the CLI's empty-glob check and the
        resolver, because rows never carry either and tab completion always adds
        the slash. (The two used to strip in DIFFERENT orders, so
        `--exclude-path '/home/z/repo/ '` kept both its slash and its space and
        slipped past both guards.)
      - **the symlink-resolved path is tested too**, because git records the
        path the worktree was created with. Resolution is best-effort: a path
        whose ancestry contains a SYMLINK LOOP contributes no resolved form
        rather than killing the scan (see `_resolve_or_none`).
      - **glob-major ORDER**: the reported glob is the first in `globs` that
        matches ANY candidate — not the first candidate's match. Pinned by
        `test_the_credited_glob_is_glob_major_not_candidate_major`.
    A literal path containing `* ? [` does NOT match itself — `[v2]` is a
    character class. Use `glob.escape`; `--help` says so.
    The cost of all this is that a glob is easier to write too WIDE than too
    narrow, and too wide is safe in this tool's only direction: it excludes MORE
    from removal, never less.
  * The default agent-worktree exclusion is exactly
    `--exclude-path '*/.claude/worktrees/*'` — the same constant fed to the same
    predicate, so the common case does not depend on the operator retyping the
    glob correctly. Note `worktrees/*`, not `worktrees/agent-*`: see the
    constant's own comment for the measurement.

🔴 WHAT THIS TOOL WILL NOT DO
-----------------------------
  * `--dry-run` is the DEFAULT. Nothing is removed without `--execute`.
  * `--execute` additionally requires `--confirm N` where N is EXACTLY the
     number of rows the run would remove. A scope that moved under you (a
     branch merged, a worktree went dirty) changes N and the run refuses.
  * Only rows whose verdict is `dead` are ever removable. `orphan` and
    `cannot-tell` are not, by construction.
  * A row matching an exclusion glob (typed OR the default agent one) is never
    removable, whatever else is true of it. That is enforced in TWO independent
    places — as a blocker in `classify()`, and re-asserted in
    `execute_removals()` immediately before the destructive step rather than
    trusted from the flag it was handed.
  * `git worktree remove` is called WITHOUT `--force`, ever. Git's own refusal
    on a dirty tree is a second, independent floor under the dirty check this
    tool already ran — and it is re-run immediately BEFORE each removal, not
    inherited from the scan that motivated it.

USAGE
  worktree-prune --scan-root ~/workspace --scan-depth 2 [--out report.json]
  worktree-prune --repo ~/workspace/devrc --format json
  worktree-prune --repos-from repos.txt --no-gh
  worktree-prune --scan-root ~/workspace --exclude-path '*/civitai/*'
  worktree-prune --repo ~/workspace/devrc --exclude-path '*/civitai/*' \\
                 --allow-unmatched-globs          # fleet list, narrow scan
  worktree-prune --scan-root ~/workspace --execute --confirm 12   # fires
  worktree-prune --scan-root ~/workspace --include-agent-worktrees  # DANGEROUS
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fnmatch
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
from pathlib import Path

# ── exit codes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RC_OK = 0
RC_USAGE = 2
RC_EXECUTE_REFUSED = 3
RC_NOTHING_TO_SCAN = 4

# Verdict vocabulary. Spelled as literals so a test can pin them without reading
# them back out of the classifier that produces them.
DEAD = "dead"
ORPHAN = "orphan"
LIVE = "live"
CANNOT_TELL = "cannot-tell"
VERDICTS = (DEAD, ORPHAN, LIVE, CANNOT_TELL)

# 🔴 The one glob behind the DEFAULT agent-worktree exclusion. Spelled ONCE: the
# default is implemented by appending this to the `--exclude-path` list, so both
# routes go through the same predicate and cannot drift apart.
#
# 🔴 Because it is a CONSTANT it cannot be mistyped, and it is therefore never a
# candidate for the zero-match `--execute` refusal below. Scoping that refusal to
# every glob rather than to operator-TYPED ones made the safety default block
# itself on any repo with no `.claude/worktrees` directory.
#
# 🔴 It is `.../worktrees/*`, NOT `.../worktrees/agent-*`. Measured 2026-08-27:
# of the 246 `.claude/worktrees/` entries this box's scan returned, TWO did not
# carry the `agent-` prefix — `…/fast/comfyui/.claude/worktrees/card-ux` (a real
# registered worktree on `refs/heads/feat/prefopt-card-ux`, `live`) and
# `…/promptver/.claude/worktrees/fix-0421-image-edit-auth` (`cannot-tell`). Under
# the narrower glob the first was spared only by the dirty check (three untracked
# `__pycache__` dirs); one `git clean` plus a squash merge and it would have
# become a removable row that the exclusion's own help text advertised as
# covered. A guard whose DESCRIPTION is wider than its BODY reads as coverage
# while providing none. The count MOVES — it was 1 of 246 a day earlier — which
# is itself the argument: the prefix is a convention nobody enforces, the
# directory is what identifies these trees.
AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB = "*/.claude/worktrees/*"

# 🔴 argv on Linux is capped (~128 KiB for a single argument list, and a
# civitai branch can touch thousands of paths). Pathspecs are therefore batched
# well under that rather than passed in one go — a truncated-argv failure would
# show up as "no differing paths", i.e. as a confident, wrong `dead`.
PATHSPEC_BATCH_BYTES = 40_000

GIT_TIMEOUT = 180
GH_TIMEOUT = 120

DEFAULT_BRANCH_CANDIDATES = ("main", "master", "trunk", "develop")


def _log(msg: str) -> None:
    print(msg, file=sys.stderr)


# ── git plumbing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def _git(cwd: Path, *args: str, timeout: int = GIT_TIMEOUT) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """One git invocation. `--no-optional-locks` so a read cannot race a
    concurrent session by refreshing the index of a repo it does not own."""
    return subprocess.run(
        ["git", "--no-optional-locks", "-C", str(cwd), *args],
        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, check=False,
    )


def _git_ok(cwd: Path, *args: str) -> "str | None":
    r = _git(cwd, *args)
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None


def is_dir(p: Path) -> bool:
    """`Path.is_dir()` that cannot raise.

    🔴 `Path.is_dir()` RAISES on EACCES — it swallows only ENOENT/ENOTDIR/EBADF/
    ELOOP. Measured on this tool's FIRST real run: a `lost+found` inside a scan
    root took the whole scan down with a PermissionError traceback before one
    row was printed. A directory we cannot read holds no worktrees we can
    classify; that is a row we cannot tell about, not a reason to abandon the
    other 750. One helper, used at every such test, because the same
    `Path.is_dir()` sits behind `path_exists` and the dirty check too.
    """
    try:
        return p.is_dir()
    except OSError:
        return False


def discover_repos(roots: "list[Path]", depth: int) -> "list[Path]":
    """Directories under `roots` (to `depth` levels) whose `.git` is a DIRECTORY.

    🔴 The `.git`-is-a-directory test is what separates a MAIN checkout from a
    linked worktree: a linked worktree's `.git` is a FILE holding `gitdir: …`.
    Enumerating worktrees from a linked worktree would report the same set N
    times and make every count wrong.
    """
    found: dict[str, Path] = {}

    def walk(d: Path, level: int) -> None:
        if level > depth:
            return
        try:
            entries = sorted(p for p in d.iterdir() if is_dir(p) and not p.is_symlink())
        except OSError:
            return
        for p in entries:
            if p.name == ".git":
                continue
            if is_dir(p / ".git"):
                # Best-effort resolve: an unresolvable path (symlink loop in its
                # ancestry) is still a repo, and still scannable by the path we
                # already hold. See `_resolve_or_none`.
                real = _resolve_or_none(p) or p
                found[str(real)] = real
                continue  # a main checkout; do not descend into its worktrees
            walk(p, level + 1)

    for root in roots:
        root = root.expanduser()
        if is_dir(root / ".git"):
            real = _resolve_or_none(root) or root
            found[str(real)] = real
            continue
        walk(root, 1)
    return sorted(found.values(), key=str)


def parse_worktree_porcelain(text: str) -> "list[dict]":
    """Parse `git worktree list --porcelain` into one dict per worktree.

    Blocks are blank-line separated; the FIRST block is the main worktree.
    Attribute lines are `key` or `key value`.
    """
    rows: list[dict] = []
    cur: "dict | None" = None
    for raw in text.splitlines():
        line = raw.rstrip("\n")
        if not line.strip():
            if cur is not None:
                rows.append(cur)
                cur = None
            continue
        key, _, value = line.partition(" ")
        if key == "worktree":
            if cur is not None:
                rows.append(cur)
            cur = {"path": value, "head": None, "branch": None, "detached": False,
                   "bare": False, "locked": False, "lock_reason": None,
                   "prunable": False, "prune_reason": None}
        elif cur is None:
            continue
        elif key == "HEAD":
            cur["head"] = value
        elif key == "branch":
            cur["branch"] = value[len("refs/heads/"):] if value.startswith("refs/heads/") else value
        elif key == "detached":
            cur["detached"] = True
        elif key == "bare":
            cur["bare"] = True
        elif key == "locked":
            cur["locked"] = True
            cur["lock_reason"] = value or None
        elif key == "prunable":
            cur["prunable"] = True
            cur["prune_reason"] = value or None
    if cur is not None:
        rows.append(cur)
    for i, r in enumerate(rows):
        r["is_main"] = (i == 0)
    return rows


def list_worktrees(repo: Path) -> "list[dict]":
    r = _git(repo, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return []
    return parse_worktree_porcelain(r.stdout)


def resolve_default_ref(repo: Path) -> "tuple[str | None, str]":
    """The repo's default branch, plus HOW it was resolved.

    The `how` string is carried into every row's evidence: a verdict computed
    against the wrong default branch is wrong in the dangerous direction, and
    "we guessed `main` because origin/HEAD was unset" must be visible.
    """
    sym = _git_ok(repo, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD")
    if sym:
        return sym, "origin-head-symref"
    for name in DEFAULT_BRANCH_CANDIDATES:
        ref = f"refs/remotes/origin/{name}"
        if _git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref).returncode == 0:
            return ref, f"remote-candidate:{name}"
    for name in DEFAULT_BRANCH_CANDIDATES:
        ref = f"refs/heads/{name}"
        if _git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref).returncode == 0:
            return ref, f"local-candidate:{name}"
    return None, "unresolved"


def repo_slug(repo: Path) -> "str | None":
    """`owner/name` from origin's URL, for `gh --repo`. None if not GitHub."""
    url = _git_ok(repo, "remote", "get-url", "origin")
    if not url:
        return None
    u = url.strip()
    if u.endswith(".git"):
        u = u[:-4]
    if "github.com" not in u:
        return None
    if u.startswith("git@") or u.startswith("ssh://"):
        _, _, tail = u.partition("github.com")
        tail = tail.lstrip(":/")
    else:
        _, _, tail = u.partition("github.com")
        tail = tail.lstrip("/")
    parts = [p for p in tail.split("/") if p]
    return "/".join(parts[:2]) if len(parts) >= 2 else None


def worktree_dirty(path: Path) -> "tuple[bool | None, int, list[str]]":
    """(dirty, count, sample). `None` means UNKNOWABLE — never False.

    Untracked files count as dirty on purpose: an unstaged new file is exactly
    the "docs written into a working tree are unsaved work" case, and it is the
    one a tracked-only check would silently discard.
    """
    if not is_dir(path):
        return None, 0, []
    r = _git(path, "status", "--porcelain=v1", "--untracked-files=normal")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, 0, [f"git status failed rc={r.returncode}: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}"]
    lines = [ln for ln in r.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    return (len(lines) > 0), len(lines), lines[:10]


def _paths_differ(repo: Path, rev_a: str, rev_b: str, paths: "list[str]") -> "bool | None":
    """Do any of `paths` differ between the two revs? None on a git failure.

    🔴 `paths` MUST be non-empty. A pathspec-less `git diff` compares the whole
    trees, which would answer a different question and — for a branch with no
    unique content — answer it wrongly. The caller short-circuits that case; the
    assertion here is the second floor under it.
    """
    if not paths:
        raise AssertionError(
            "_paths_differ called with an EMPTY pathspec — that silently widens "
            "the comparison to the whole tree. The no-content-change case is "
            "the caller's to handle.")
    batch: list[str] = []
    size = 0
    batches: list[list[str]] = []
    for p in paths:
        if batch and size + len(p) + 1 > PATHSPEC_BATCH_BYTES:
            batches.append(batch)
            batch, size = [], 0
        batch.append(p)
        size += len(p) + 1
    if batch:
        batches.append(batch)
    for b in batches:
        r = _git(repo, "diff", "--name-only", rev_a, rev_b, "--", *b)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            return None
        if r.stdout.strip():
            return True
    return False


def landing_signals(repo: Path, head: str, default_ref: str) -> "dict":
    """Which of the git-side landing signals fired, and what was checked.

    Returns {"fired": [...], "checked": [...], "notes": [...]}.
    """
    fired: list[str] = []
    checked: list[str] = []
    notes: list[str] = []

    checked.append("ancestor")
    anc = _git(repo, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", head, default_ref)
    if anc.returncode == 0:
        fired.append("ancestor")
        notes.append(f"{head[:12]} is an ancestor of {default_ref}")
        return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes}
    if anc.returncode not in (0, 1):
        notes.append(f"ancestor check failed rc={anc.returncode}: {anc.stderr.strip()[:160]}")
        return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes, "error": True}

    base = _git_ok(repo, "merge-base", head, default_ref)
    if not base:
        notes.append(f"no merge-base between {head[:12]} and {default_ref}")
        return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes, "error": True}

    checked.append("content-identical")
    r = _git(repo, "diff", "--name-only", base, head)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        notes.append(f"changed-path listing failed rc={r.returncode}")
        return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes, "error": True}
    changed = [p for p in r.stdout.splitlines() if p.strip()]
    if not changed:
        # 🔴 The empty-operand case, handled here and NOT by a pathspec-less
        # diff. The branch introduced no content relative to its merge-base, so
        # there is nothing that could fail to be present upstream.
        fired.append("no-content-change")
        notes.append(f"branch changes no path relative to merge-base {base[:12]}")
        return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes}
    differ = _paths_differ(repo, head, default_ref, changed)
    if differ is None:
        notes.append("content comparison failed")
        return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes, "error": True}
    if not differ:
        fired.append("content-identical")
        notes.append(f"all {len(changed)} changed path(s) have identical content in {default_ref}")
    else:
        notes.append(f"at least one of {len(changed)} changed path(s) differs from {default_ref}")

    checked.append("patch-equivalent")
    cherry = _git(repo, "cherry", default_ref, head)
    if cherry.returncode == 0:
        lines = [ln for ln in cherry.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip()]
        if lines and all(ln.startswith("-") for ln in lines):
            fired.append("patch-equivalent")
            notes.append(f"git cherry: all {len(lines)} commit(s) have an equivalent upstream")
        elif lines:
            unmatched = sum(1 for ln in lines if ln.startswith("+"))
            notes.append(f"git cherry: {unmatched}/{len(lines)} commit(s) have no upstream equivalent")
    else:
        notes.append(f"git cherry failed rc={cherry.returncode}")

    return {"fired": fired, "checked": checked, "notes": notes}


# ── gh / pull requests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

class PullRequestIndex:
    """Per-repo branch -> pull request state, with an explicit ANSWERED flag.

    🔴 `answered` is the whole point. "No PR found" and "we could not ask" are
    the same OBSERVABLE — an empty result — and they license opposite verdicts:
    the first supports `orphan`, the second supports only `cannot-tell`. A
    truncated PR listing collapses them too, so truncation is detected and the
    per-branch fallback query is used for anything the bulk index missed.
    """

    def __init__(self, slug: "str | None", gh_cmd: str, enabled: bool, limit: int = 2000):
        self.slug = slug
        self.gh_cmd = gh_cmd
        self.enabled = enabled
        self.limit = limit
        self.by_branch: "dict[str, dict]" = {}
        self.truncated = False
        self.available = False
        self.reason = "gh disabled by --no-gh" if not enabled else "not fetched"

    def fetch(self) -> None:
        if not self.enabled:
            return
        if not self.slug:
            self.reason = "no github origin remote — cannot ask gh"
            return
        r = self._gh("pr", "list", "--repo", self.slug, "--state", "all",
                     "--limit", str(self.limit),
                     "--json", "number,state,headRefName,mergedAt,url")
        if r is None or r.returncode != 0:
            err = "gh not runnable" if r is None else (r.stderr.strip()[:200] or f"rc={r.returncode}")
            self.reason = f"gh pr list failed: {err}"
            return
        try:
            data = json.loads(r.stdout or "[]")
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            self.reason = "gh pr list returned unparseable JSON"
            return
        for pr in data:
            branch = pr.get("headRefName")
            if not branch:
                continue
            prev = self.by_branch.get(branch)
            # Prefer MERGED > OPEN > CLOSED, then the highest number.
            rank = {"MERGED": 3, "OPEN": 2, "CLOSED": 1}
            if prev is None or (rank.get(pr.get("state"), 0), pr.get("number", 0)) > \
                    (rank.get(prev.get("state"), 0), prev.get("number", 0)):
                self.by_branch[branch] = pr
        self.truncated = len(data) >= self.limit
        self.available = True
        self.reason = (f"gh pr list returned {len(data)} PR(s)"
                       + (f" — AT THE {self.limit} LIMIT, index may be truncated" if self.truncated else ""))

    def lookup(self, branch: "str | None") -> "dict":
        """{'answered': bool, 'state': str|None, 'number': int|None, 'why': str}"""
        if branch is None:
            return {"answered": False, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": "detached HEAD — no branch to look a pull request up by"}
        if not self.enabled:
            return {"answered": False, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": "gh disabled (--no-gh)"}
        if not self.available:
            return {"answered": False, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": self.reason}
        hit = self.by_branch.get(branch)
        if hit is not None:
            return {"answered": True, "state": hit.get("state"), "number": hit.get("number"),
                    "merged_at": hit.get("mergedAt"), "why": "found in the repo's PR index"}
        if not self.truncated:
            return {"answered": True, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": f"absent from a COMPLETE index of {len(self.by_branch)} branch(es)"}
        # Truncated index: an absence proves nothing. Ask directly.
        r = self._gh("pr", "list", "--repo", self.slug, "--head", branch, "--state", "all",
                     "--limit", "10", "--json", "number,state,headRefName,mergedAt,url")
        if r is None or r.returncode != 0:
            return {"answered": False, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": "PR index truncated and the per-branch query failed"}
        try:
            data = json.loads(r.stdout or "[]")
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            return {"answered": False, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": "PR index truncated and the per-branch query returned bad JSON"}
        if not data:
            return {"answered": True, "state": None, "number": None, "merged_at": None,
                    "why": "no pull request for this branch (direct query)"}
        rank = {"MERGED": 3, "OPEN": 2, "CLOSED": 1}
        best = max(data, key=lambda p: (rank.get(p.get("state"), 0), p.get("number", 0)))
        return {"answered": True, "state": best.get("state"), "number": best.get("number"),
                "merged_at": best.get("mergedAt"), "why": "direct per-branch query"}

    def _gh(self, *args: str) -> "subprocess.CompletedProcess | None":
        try:
            return subprocess.run([self.gh_cmd, *args], capture_output=True, text=True,
                                  timeout=GH_TIMEOUT, check=False)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            return None


# ── path exclusion ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def normalize_glob(raw: str) -> str:
    """ONE spelling of "what a `--exclude-path` value actually means".

    🔴 Surrounding whitespace FIRST, then trailing slashes. Rows carry
    `str(Path(...))`, which never ends in `/`, and shell tab-completion appends
    one to every directory — so `--exclude-path ~/workspace/civitai/` would
    otherwise match nothing and look exactly like no filter.

    🔴 One function because there used to be TWO spellings that disagreed: the
    resolver did `raw.rstrip("/")` then tested `.strip()`, the CLI's empty check
    did `.strip().rstrip("/")`. `--exclude-path '/home/z/repo/ '` therefore
    passed the CLI check (which saw `/home/z/repo`) and reached the matcher with
    BOTH its slash and its trailing space still attached — a glob matching
    nothing, arriving through the very guards written to stop that.
    """
    return raw.strip().rstrip("/")


def normalize_globs(patterns: "list[str] | None") -> "list[str]":
    """The operator-TYPED globs: normalised, blanks dropped, order-preserving
    dedupe. Kept separate from the effective list because the zero-match
    `--execute` refusal applies to THESE and not to implied constants."""
    globs: list[str] = []
    for raw in (patterns or []):
        g = normalize_glob(raw)
        if not g:
            continue
        if g not in globs:
            globs.append(g)
    return globs


def agent_glob_applied_by_default(include_agent_worktrees: bool) -> bool:
    """ONE spelling of "does this tool apply `AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB` ON ITS OWN, on
    THIS command line?" — the fact every message about the constant rests on.

    🔴 THIS FUNCTION EXISTS BECAUSE THE PREDICATE WAS OPEN-CODED AT THREE SITES
    AND TWO WHOLE FIX ROUNDS ARE COPIES DIVERGING FROM EACH OTHER. Round 4 gated
    a message on the FLAG where the truth depended on this FACT; round 5 fixed
    two spellings of it and introduced two more false claims in the replacements.
    `claude/RULES.md` → "One rule, one place": a predicate open-coded at N sites
    is typically wrong at N−1 of them, and consolidating is what makes the
    disagreement audible. Pinned by
    `test_the_agent_default_predicate_has_exactly_one_definition`.

    🔴 IT IS NOT "IS THE GLOB IN FORCE". That is `agent_worktree_glob_in_force`,
    a different question with a different answer: `--include-agent-worktrees
    --exclude-path '*/.claude/worktrees/*'` leaves the glob IN FORCE (typed) while
    this predicate is False (the tool is not applying it — the operator is). The
    two were conflated once already and produced a 🔴 warning the next three
    lines of the same report contradicted.

    🔴 WHAT IT LICENSES, precisely: "you can drop your hand-typed copy of the
    constant and lose nothing", because `resolve_exclude_globs` puts it back.
    When it is False the hand-typed copy is the ONLY thing sparing agent
    worktrees, and that advice points the DELETING-MORE way — at 137 live
    sessions' working directories.
    """
    return not include_agent_worktrees


def resolve_exclude_globs(patterns: "list[str] | None",
                          include_agent_worktrees: bool) -> "list[str]":
    """The effective glob list. ONE RULE, ONE PLACE.

    The default agent-worktree exclusion is not a second matcher — it is this
    list plus `AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB`, matched by the same `path_excluded` below.
    A second open-coded match is exactly how the two would come to disagree.

    🔴 THE DEFAULT IS ON. `include_agent_worktrees=True` is the operator asking
    for the dangerous behaviour by name; there is no flag that turns the
    exclusion on, because it is not off.

    🔴 This is the site that MAKES `agent_glob_applied_by_default` true, so it
    reads that predicate rather than restating it — otherwise the definition of
    the fact and the messages describing it are two things that can drift, which
    is what happened twice.
    """
    globs = normalize_globs(patterns)
    if agent_glob_applied_by_default(include_agent_worktrees) and AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB not in globs:
        globs.append(AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB)
    return globs


def _resolve_or_none(p: Path) -> "Path | None":
    """`Path.resolve()` that cannot raise. ONE helper, every call site.

    🔴 NON-STRICT `resolve()` STILL RAISES, and not only `OSError` — and it
    raises on ONE interpreter and not the next. MEASURED at two points, on the
    same looped-symlink path, 2026-08-27:

        CPython 3.12.14 (this dev shell) -> RuntimeError("Symlink loop from …")
        CPython 3.13.15                  -> returns the path unresolved, no raise

    Name the points, because the claim is only about those two. The reason the
    `except` is WIDE rather than the 3.13 behaviour being relied on is exactly
    that split: this tool runs under whatever interpreter the shell hands it,
    and on 3.12 `path_excluded()` and `summarize()` both raise on such a path
    (measured directly) — which in a scan means one looped symlink anywhere in
    one worktree's ancestry takes down every other row with a traceback.
    `ValueError` is in the list for the embedded-NUL case, a different way for
    the same call to reject a path rather than fail on the filesystem.

    🔴 LOSING THE RESOLVED FORM IS THE **DELETING-MORE** DIRECTION, NOT THE SAFE
    ONE. An earlier revision of this docstring called it "the conservative
    direction … a row is excluded LESS readily"; for an EXCLUSION filter,
    excluding less readily is exactly the direction the safe-direction rule at
    the top of this file forbids ("excludes MORE from removal, never less").
    Said plainly: a glob that would have matched only the resolved spelling stops
    matching, and the row stops being spared by that glob.

    🔴 WHAT ACTUALLY MAKES IT SAFE is a different mechanism entirely, and it is
    worth naming because the wrong one was written down here for two rounds: the
    only paths that reach the `None` branch are paths whose ancestry cannot be
    walked, and NOTHING can stat such a path. `is_dir()` is False, so the row
    carries `path_exists=False`; git cannot stat it either, so it is reported
    `prunable`. Either lands `cannot-tell` in `classify()` — measured, BOTH are
    set on a real fixture and `prunable` is the branch that fires first — and a
    `cannot-tell` row is NEVER removable, for a reason that has nothing to do
    with globs. The exclusion degrading is therefore moot on precisely the rows
    that can trigger it. Pinned by
    `test_a_row_whose_resolve_fails_is_cannot_tell_not_removable`.
    """
    try:
        return p.resolve()
    except (OSError, RuntimeError, ValueError):
        return None


def _path_candidates(path: str) -> "list[str]":
    """Every string a glob is tested against for one worktree path.

    Three widenings, each closing a way a filter could silently fail to match:

    * the path AS GIT RECORDED IT — the primary form;
    * its SYMLINK-RESOLVED form, because git records the path the worktree was
      created with. An operator who globs the real path while git holds the
      symlinked one (or vice versa) would otherwise get a silent zero;
    * every ANCESTOR of both. This is what makes `--exclude-path <dir>` cover
      the directory's CONTENTS. Without it a glob naming a repo matched the
      repo's own row and nothing else — printing a reassuring
      "1 row(s) matched … 0 of them are dead" while both dead children stayed
      removable. A reassuring positive is worse than a silent zero.

    🔴 Resolution is BEST-EFFORT (`_resolve_or_none`). Before path exclusion
    existed this function did not touch the filesystem at all, so no path's
    SHAPE could kill a scan; it can now, and a scan that dies on row 12 of 860
    is a worse outcome than a row with one candidate form fewer.
    """
    forms = [path]
    resolved = _resolve_or_none(Path(path))
    real = str(resolved) if resolved is not None else None
    if real and real != path:
        forms.append(real)
    out: list[str] = []
    for f in forms:
        p = Path(f)
        for cand in (str(p), *(str(a) for a in p.parents)):
            if cand not in out:
                out.append(cand)
    return out


def path_excluded(path: str, globs: "list[str] | None") -> "str | None":
    """The FIRST glob in `globs` that matches `path`, or None.

    Returning the matching glob rather than a bool is deliberate: the row records
    WHY it was spared, so a report reader can tell a deliberate exclusion from a
    verdict — and can see which of several globs did it.

    🔴 SEMANTICS, all four pinned by tests:
      1. `fnmatch.fnmatchcase`, so `*` CROSSES `/` — this is not shell or
         `pathlib` globbing (`test_the_glob_star_crosses_slashes`).
      2. `fnmatchcase` rather than `fnmatch`, so matching never routes through
         `os.path.normcase` and cannot start case-folding on a different
         platform (`test_the_matcher_does_not_route_through_normcase`).
      3. SUBTREE semantics: a glob that matches any ANCESTOR of the worktree
         excludes it too, so naming a directory covers everything under it
         (`test_excluding_a_directory_covers_its_contents`).
      4. Both the recorded and the symlink-resolved path are tested
         (`test_a_glob_on_the_resolved_path_matches_a_symlinked_repo`).
      5. 🔴 GLOB-MAJOR, not candidate-major. The loops below are nested glob
         OUTSIDE, candidate INSIDE, so "first" means first in `globs` — a later
         glob matching the path ITSELF does not outrank an earlier glob matching
         an ANCESTOR. `path_excluded('/a/b/c', ['/a/b', '*/c'])` is `'/a/b'`.
         Swapping the nesting returns `'*/c'` and SURVIVED the whole suite,
         because the only ordering test used direct-path matches, where the two
         nestings cannot disagree
         (`test_the_credited_glob_is_glob_major_not_candidate_major`).
         Only the CREDITED glob moves — per-glob match counts are computed one
         glob at a time and are independent of this.

    🔴 A literal path containing an `fnmatch` metacharacter (`* ? [`) does NOT
    match itself — `fnmatchcase('/a/proj[v2]', '/a/proj[v2]')` is False, because
    `[v2]` is a character class. Run such a path through `glob.escape` first;
    `--help` says so.
    """
    if not globs:
        return None
    candidates = _path_candidates(path)
    # 🔴 glob-major: `g` OUTSIDE, `c` inside. See semantic 5 above.
    for g in globs:
        for c in candidates:
            if fnmatch.fnmatchcase(c, g):
                return g
    return None


# ── classification ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def classify(row: "dict") -> "dict":
    """Decide the verdict from the already-gathered evidence on `row`.

    Pure: it runs no subprocess, so a test can drive every branch from a dict.
    """
    ev: list[str] = list(row.get("evidence", []))
    blockers: list[str] = []

    def out(verdict: str, reason: str) -> dict:
        row["verdict"] = verdict
        row["verdict_reason"] = reason
        row["evidence"] = ev
        removable = verdict == DEAD and not blockers
        row["removable"] = removable
        row["blockers"] = blockers
        return row

    # 🔴 FIRST, and unconditional: an excluded row is spared whatever verdict the
    # evidence goes on to support. Placed here rather than in each branch so
    # there is exactly one statement to read (and exactly one to break).
    excluded_by = row.get("excluded_by")
    if excluded_by:
        blockers.append(f"excluded by path filter {excluded_by!r} — reported, never removed")

    if row.get("is_main"):
        blockers.append("main checkout — never a removal candidate")
        return out(LIVE, "the repository's main worktree")
    if row.get("bare"):
        blockers.append("bare worktree")
        return out(LIVE, "bare worktree")

    if row.get("prunable"):
        blockers.append("git reports this worktree as prunable (stale admin entry)")
        return out(CANNOT_TELL,
                   "git reports the worktree prunable: "
                   f"{row.get('prune_reason') or 'no reason given'} — "
                   "`git worktree prune` is the right tool, not `remove`")
    if not row.get("path_exists"):
        blockers.append("worktree path does not exist on disk")
        return out(CANNOT_TELL, "the worktree directory is gone but git still lists it")

    if row.get("locked"):
        blockers.append("worktree is locked")
        return out(LIVE, f"locked: {row.get('lock_reason') or 'no reason given'}")

    dirty = row.get("dirty")
    if dirty is None:
        blockers.append("could not determine whether the worktree is dirty")
        return out(CANNOT_TELL, "`git status` did not answer — dirtiness unknown")
    if dirty:
        blockers.append(f"{row.get('dirty_count', 0)} uncommitted/untracked path(s)")
        return out(LIVE, f"uncommitted work: {row.get('dirty_count', 0)} dirty path(s)")

    if row.get("default_ref") is None:
        blockers.append("default branch could not be resolved")
        return out(CANNOT_TELL, "no default branch to compare against")

    pr = row.get("pr") or {}
    if pr.get("state") == "OPEN":
        blockers.append(f"open pull request #{pr.get('number')}")
        return out(LIVE, f"open pull request #{pr.get('number')}")

    fired = list(row.get("landed_signals") or [])
    if pr.get("state") == "MERGED":
        if "pr-merged" not in fired:
            fired.append("pr-merged")
            row["landed_signals"] = fired
        ev.append(f"pr-merged: #{pr.get('number')} merged at {pr.get('merged_at')}")
    if fired:
        return out(DEAD, "landed — signals: " + ", ".join(fired))

    if row.get("signal_error"):
        blockers.append("a git containment check failed")
        return out(CANNOT_TELL, "a containment check errored; landing is undetermined")

    if pr.get("answered"):
        state = pr.get("state")
        detail = f"closed-unmerged PR #{pr.get('number')}" if state == "CLOSED" else "no pull request"
        return out(ORPHAN,
                   f"no landing signal fired and {detail} ({pr.get('why')}) — "
                   "unmerged work with nothing tracking it")

    blockers.append("no authoritative pull-request answer")
    return out(CANNOT_TELL,
               "no landing signal fired, and the pull-request state is UNKNOWN "
               f"({pr.get('why')}) — an absence of evidence, not evidence of absence")


# ── scanning ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def scan_repo(repo: Path, gh_cmd: str, use_gh: bool, pr_limit: int, jobs: int,
              exclude_globs: "list[str] | None" = None) -> "list[dict]":
    wts = list_worktrees(repo)
    if not wts:
        return []
    default_ref, default_how = resolve_default_ref(repo)
    slug = repo_slug(repo)
    index = PullRequestIndex(slug, gh_cmd, use_gh, pr_limit)
    index.fetch()

    def build(wt: dict) -> dict:
        path = Path(wt["path"])
        row: dict = {
            "repo": str(repo),
            "repo_slug": slug,
            "path": str(path),
            "path_exists": is_dir(path),
            "is_main": wt["is_main"],
            "bare": wt["bare"],
            "detached": wt["detached"],
            "branch": wt["branch"],
            "head": wt["head"],
            "locked": wt["locked"],
            "lock_reason": wt["lock_reason"],
            "prunable": wt["prunable"],
            "prune_reason": wt["prune_reason"],
            "default_ref": default_ref,
            "default_how": default_how,
            "excluded_by": path_excluded(str(path), exclude_globs),
            "evidence": [f"default branch {default_ref} resolved by {default_how}"],
        }
        if row["excluded_by"]:
            # Recorded as EVIDENCE, not as a verdict: the row is still fully
            # classified below and still printed. Only its removability changes.
            row["evidence"].append(
                f"excluded by --exclude-path {row['excluded_by']!r} — "
                "classified and reported, but never removable")
        dirty, count, sample = worktree_dirty(path)
        row["dirty"] = dirty
        row["dirty_count"] = count
        row["dirty_sample"] = sample
        row["evidence"].append(
            "dirty=UNKNOWN" if dirty is None else f"dirty={dirty} ({count} path(s))")

        row["pr"] = index.lookup(wt["branch"])
        row["evidence"].append(
            f"pr: answered={row['pr']['answered']} state={row['pr']['state']} ({row['pr']['why']})")

        row["landed_signals"] = []
        row["checks_run"] = []
        row["signal_error"] = False
        if (not wt["is_main"] and not wt["bare"] and default_ref and wt["head"]
                and dirty is False and row["pr"].get("state") != "OPEN"):
            sig = landing_signals(repo, wt["head"], default_ref)
            row["landed_signals"] = sig["fired"]
            row["checks_run"] = sig["checked"]
            row["signal_error"] = bool(sig.get("error"))
            row["evidence"].extend(sig["notes"])
        return classify(row)

    if jobs > 1:
        with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=jobs) as pool:
            return list(pool.map(build, wts))
    return [build(w) for w in wts]


# ── reporting ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def summarize(rows: "list[dict]", exclude_globs: "list[str] | None" = None,
              typed_globs: "list[str] | None" = None, *,
              agent_worktrees_included: bool,
              allow_unmatched_globs: bool = False) -> "dict":
    """The report's numbers, INCLUDING a per-glob match count.

    🔴 The glob accounting lives here rather than being bolted on by `main()`
    afterwards, so the summary dict has ONE shape whatever the caller — and so
    that no caller can render a report without it.

    🔴 Each glob is counted INDEPENDENTLY (`path_excluded(path, [g])`), not by
    tallying `excluded_by`. `excluded_by` records the FIRST glob that matched, so
    a working glob shadowed by an earlier one would tally zero and raise a false
    alarm. The question this count answers is "does THIS glob match anything",
    and shadowing is not part of it.

    🔴 `typed_globs` is the subset of `exclude_globs` the OPERATOR typed. Only
    those can block `--execute`, because only those can be mistyped; an implied
    constant matching zero rows is an ordinary, expected observation. Omitting
    the argument treats every glob as typed — the conservative default, since it
    can only produce MORE refusals, and `--allow-unmatched-globs` now exists to
    get past one.

    🔴 `agent_worktrees_included` IS REQUIRED AND KEYWORD-ONLY. It used to default
    to False, and the default was a lie a caller could not see: the glob list is
    resolved by `resolve_exclude_globs(patterns, include_agent_worktrees)`, so a
    caller that opted the flag ON there and then omitted it here got
    `agent_worktree_glob_in_force=False` alongside `agent_glob_applied_by_default
    =True` — a summary asserting the tool applies a constant that is not in the
    list, which is the exact conflation this PR's rounds 4-6 are about.
    `main()` always passed it, so nothing reachable from a command line could
    produce that shape; the README publishes this JSON for consumers, and a
    default that can only ever be wrong when it is used is not a convenience.
    """
    counts = {v: 0 for v in VERDICTS}
    for r in rows:
        counts[r["verdict"]] = counts.get(r["verdict"], 0) + 1
    excluded = [r for r in rows if r.get("excluded_by")]

    globs = list(exclude_globs or [])
    typed = set(globs if typed_globs is None else typed_globs)
    per_glob: list[dict] = []
    for g in globs:
        hits = [r for r in rows if path_excluded(r["path"], [g])]
        per_glob.append({
            "glob": g,
            # 🔴 Whether the OPERATOR typed this glob, not whether it is in
            # force. It decides refusal eligibility and how the glob is labelled
            # in the report, and it is in the JSON so a consumer can tell an
            # operator filter from the tool's own default.
            "typed": g in typed,
            "matched": len(hits),
            "matched_dead": sum(1 for r in hits if r["verdict"] == DEAD),
        })

    return {
        "worktrees": len(rows),
        "repos": len({r["repo"] for r in rows}),
        "counts": counts,
        "removable": sum(1 for r in rows if r.get("removable")),
        "excluded": len(excluded),
        # How many rows the exclusion actually SPARED — i.e. would have been
        # removed without it. That is the number that says whether the filter did
        # anything, and it is not the same as `excluded`.
        "excluded_dead": sum(1 for r in excluded if r["verdict"] == DEAD),
        "exclude_globs": per_glob,
        # 🔴 THE HEADLINE SAFETY NUMBER. A glob that matches nothing produces
        # output BYTE-IDENTICAL to passing no filter at all, and `--confirm N`
        # offers zero independent protection because N is derived from the same
        # wrong count. So the zero-matchers are named, not merely implied by a
        # count the operator would have to notice was missing.
        #
        # EVERY zero-matcher, typed or implied — this key's name is its claim.
        "exclude_globs_matching_nothing": [d["glob"] for d in per_glob if d["matched"] == 0],
        # 🔴 The strictly narrower list `--execute` actually refuses on: the
        # TYPED zero-matchers. A hardcoded constant cannot be mistyped, so it
        # carries none of the hazard the refusal was built for — and scoping the
        # refusal to every glob made the default safety exclusion refuse ITSELF
        # on any repo with no `.claude/worktrees` directory, leaving "turn the
        # safety off" as the only way through.
        "exclude_globs_blocking_execute": [
            d["glob"] for d in per_glob if d["matched"] == 0 and d["typed"]],
        # Which non-default choices were in force, so the report can say so and
        # a JSON consumer can see it. An override invisible in the output is a
        # hardcode wearing a costume.
        "agent_worktrees_included": bool(agent_worktrees_included),
        "allow_unmatched_globs": bool(allow_unmatched_globs),
        # 🔴 Whether the agent glob is ACTUALLY OUT OF FORCE — which is not the
        # same question as whether the flag was passed, and the report used to
        # ask the wrong one. `--include-agent-worktrees --exclude-path
        # '*/.claude/worktrees/*'` drops the default and re-adds the identical
        # glob as a TYPED one, so agent worktrees are still spared; the report
        # nonetheless shouted that they were "ordinary removal candidates" three
        # lines above its own filter table saying they had been spared. A
        # 🔴-marked warning that the next lines of the same report contradict is
        # how a real one gets tuned out.
        #
        # Deliberately an EXACT membership test on the constant, not an attempt
        # to decide whether some other typed glob happens to cover agent trees.
        # A near-miss glob (`*/.claude/worktrees/agent-*` covers less) still gets
        # the shout, which is the right way for this to be wrong.
        "agent_worktree_glob_in_force": AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB in globs,
        # 🔴 THE ONE COMPUTATION of "is the constant applied by this tool on its
        # own?", carried in the summary so no message site ever re-derives it.
        # Every sentence that offers "drop your hand-typed copy" as a free remedy
        # reads THIS key; `render_text` and `main` each used to spell it out for
        # themselves, and rounds 4 and 5 are what that cost. Distinct from
        # `agent_worktree_glob_in_force` directly above — see
        # `agent_glob_applied_by_default` for why conflating them is the bug.
        "agent_glob_applied_by_default": agent_glob_applied_by_default(agent_worktrees_included),
    }


def verdict_label(row: "dict") -> str:
    """The verdict as PRINTED. An excluded row keeps its verdict and gains a
    marker — it must not read as a fifth verdict, and must not read as an
    ordinary `dead` either."""
    return f"{row['verdict']} (excluded)" if row.get("excluded_by") else row["verdict"]


def render_text(rows: "list[dict]", summary: dict, verbose: bool) -> str:
    lines: list[str] = []
    per_repo: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for r in rows:
        d = per_repo.setdefault(r["repo"], {v: 0 for v in VERDICTS})
        d[r["verdict"]] += 1
        d["excluded"] = d.get("excluded", 0) + (1 if r.get("excluded_by") else 0)
    lines.append(f"{'repo':<58} {'dead':>5} {'orphan':>7} {'live':>5} {'cannot-tell':>12} "
                 f"{'excluded':>9}")
    for repo in sorted(per_repo):
        d = per_repo[repo]
        lines.append(f"{repo[-58:]:<58} {d[DEAD]:>5} {d[ORPHAN]:>7} {d[LIVE]:>5} "
                     f"{d[CANNOT_TELL]:>12} {d.get('excluded', 0):>9}")
    c = summary["counts"]
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(f"{summary['worktrees']} worktree(s) across {summary['repos']} repo(s): "
                 f"{c[DEAD]} dead, {c[ORPHAN]} orphan, {c[LIVE]} live, {c[CANNOT_TELL]} cannot-tell")
    lines.append(f"{summary['removable']} row(s) would be removed by --execute "
                 f"(dead + clean + unlocked only)")
    # 🔴 IMMEDIATELY under the removable count, because that is the number an
    # operator reads as "everything this run covers". With the agent-worktree
    # exclusion ON BY DEFAULT the removable count is routinely far short of the
    # dead count, and a summary that let it read as complete coverage is the
    # silent-truncation failure this tool exists not to have.
    if summary.get("excluded_dead"):
        lines.append(
            f"🔴 …and {summary['excluded_dead']} further `dead` row(s) were SPARED by an "
            f"exclusion filter — NOT in the number above, and NOT covered by this run. "
            f"They are listed under `exclusion filters in force` below; --verbose gives "
            f"their paths.")
    # 🔴 The dangerous choice is the one you have to type — so say, loudly, when
    # it was typed. Printed before the filter list because it is the reason the
    # list is one line shorter than it usually is.
    #
    # 🔴 GATED ON THE GLOB, NOT ON THE FLAG. It used to fire on the flag alone
    # and could then be FALSE three lines above the filter table that
    # contradicted it — see `agent_worktree_glob_in_force` in `summarize`.
    if summary.get("agent_worktrees_included") and not summary.get("agent_worktree_glob_in_force"):
        lines.append(
            f"🔴 --include-agent-worktrees IS IN FORCE: the default exclusion "
            f"{AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB!r} is OFF for this run, so worktrees belonging to OTHER "
            f"LIVE Claude sessions are ordinary removal candidates. Removing one destroys a "
            f"working directory that is in use RIGHT NOW. Drop the flag unless you have "
            f"checked every path by hand.")
    elif summary.get("agent_worktrees_included"):
        # 🔴 "STILL spared" is the whole claim, and it is the only one this note
        # is entitled to make. An earlier wording went further — "behaves exactly
        # like the default … the flag is doing nothing here" — and that was
        # MEASURABLY false in the direction that matters: typing the glob turns
        # the constant from an IMPLIED glob into a TYPED one, and only typed
        # globs are `--execute` refusal candidates
        # (`exclude_globs_blocking_execute`). So on a scan holding no agent
        # worktrees the true default proceeds (RC_OK, rows removed) and this
        # command line REFUSES — a difference the note was printing on the stdout
        # of the very run whose stderr said REFUSED.
        #
        # 🔴 …AND THE LAST CLAUSE IS ITSELF CONDITIONAL, which round 5 missed —
        # reintroducing the round-4 defect verbatim inside the sentence written
        # to replace it. `--allow-unmatched-globs` downgrades that refusal to a
        # warning (`main`), so under all three flags the run REMOVES and exits
        # RC_OK while this line said "this run REFUSES". MEASURED, hermetic
        # tmpdir, before the fix: rc 0, the worktree gone from disk, and the
        # claim on stdout three lines above the report's own
        # "--allow-unmatched-globs IS IN FORCE: … it does not refuse --execute".
        # An operator who reads "REFUSES" treats the run as inert and stops
        # re-reading `--confirm N`.
        # 🔴 THREE-WAY, BECAUSE THE REFUSAL HAS THREE SCOPINGS AND NOT TWO.
        # Round 6 made this conditional on `--allow-unmatched-globs` and stopped
        # there — but `main()` declines to refuse on a THIRD condition too (the
        # scan produced NO ROWS), and on that command line this note still said
        # "this run REFUSES" over a run that returned RC_OK. Same defect as
        # round 5's, third exemption. MEASURED, hermetic tmpdir:
        # `--repo <nonexistent> --include-agent-worktrees --exclude-path
        # '*/.claude/worktrees/*' --execute --confirm 0` -> rc 0, stderr
        # "…this scan produced NO rows at all … Not refusing.", stdout
        # "this run REFUSES". Bounded (zero rows ⇒ zero removals) but it is the
        # exact class this round exists to close.
        #
        # 🟡 AND THE WORDING IS SCOPED TO THE REFUSAL, NEVER TO WHAT IS REMOVED.
        # The round-6 text ended "…it does NOT refuse and this run removes
        # exactly what the default would have removed"; ONE extra --exclude-path
        # falsifies it. MEASURED on a one-repo/one-dead-worktree fixture:
        # `--include-agent-worktrees --exclude-path <agent glob> --exclude-path
        # '*/wts/*' --allow-unmatched-globs --execute --confirm 0` removed 0
        # while the bare default `--execute --confirm 1` removed 1 — and the
        # parity claim printed on the first run's stdout. The flag decides
        # REFUSAL ELIGIBILITY; the glob list decides removals. Under-deleting, so
        # nothing was at risk, but a newly written false sentence about the run
        # printing it is the whole subject of this PR.
        if not summary.get("worktrees"):
            differs = ("does NEITHER: this scan produced no rows at all, so nothing could "
                       "have matched, the zero says nothing about the glob, and --execute "
                       "refuses on neither command line")
        elif summary.get("allow_unmatched_globs"):
            differs = ("WARNS where the default would have said nothing — "
                       "--allow-unmatched-globs is in force, so it does NOT refuse. That is a "
                       "claim about the REFUSAL and nothing else: what gets removed is decided "
                       "by the globs in force, so any other --exclude-path you typed can still "
                       "spare rows the bare default would have removed")
        else:
            differs = ("can REFUSE --execute on that glob, which the tool's own implied copy "
                       "never could — though whether the same command line WITHOUT the flag "
                       "also refuses depends on the other globs you typed")
        lines.append(
            f"note: --include-agent-worktrees was passed, but {AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB!r} is in "
            f"force anyway as a --exclude-path you typed — so agent worktrees are STILL "
            f"spared and this run can remove no more than the bare default would. It is NOT "
            f"identical to the default, though: a glob YOU typed is an --execute refusal "
            f"candidate, so on a scan holding no agent worktrees this run "
            f"{differs}.")
    # 🔴 Printed whenever a glob is in force — never gated on whether anything
    # matched. Gating it on the match count is precisely the bug: a mistyped glob
    # then produced output identical to an unfiltered run, and the operator read
    # the full removable count as if their filter were in force.
    if summary.get("exclude_globs"):
        lines.append("")
        lines.append(f"exclusion filters in force ({len(summary['exclude_globs'])}):")
        for d in summary["exclude_globs"]:
            origin = ("--exclude-path" if d.get("typed")
                      else "(default; --include-agent-worktrees turns it off)")
            lines.append(f"    {origin} {d['glob']!r}  ->  matched {d['matched']} row(s), "
                         f"{d['matched_dead']} of them dead")
        lines.append(
            f"{summary['excluded']} row(s) matched at least one glob — still classified and "
            f"counted above, NEVER removed, and NOT counted toward --confirm. "
            f"{summary['excluded_dead']} of them are `dead` and would otherwise have been "
            f"removed. Re-run with --verbose to list them by path.")
        # 🔴 Only the TYPED zero-matchers get the shout. The default constant
        # matching zero rows just means this scan holds no agent worktrees —
        # shouting about that is how a real warning gets tuned out, and refusing
        # on it made the safety default block itself.
        for g in summary.get("exclude_globs_blocking_execute") or []:
            # 🔴 FLAG vs FACT, in the sentence that offers a REMEDY — and the
            # remedy points the DELETING-MORE way when it is wrong. "Drop it and
            # lose nothing" is true only while the constant is ACTUALLY applied,
            # i.e. while `--include-agent-worktrees` was NOT passed. With the
            # flag in force `resolve_exclude_globs` does not append the constant,
            # so this hand-typed copy is the ONLY thing sparing agent worktrees
            # on that command line; advising the operator to drop it while they
            # keep the flag is how 137 live sessions' working directories become
            # removable rows. Gated on the same key the round-4 shout fix
            # introduced, because it is the same distinction — and it now READS
            # that distinction from the summary instead of re-deriving it, which
            # is what stops a seventh round of this class.
            #
            # 🟢 No `normalize_glob(g)` here: `g` comes from
            # `exclude_globs_blocking_execute`, which is built from
            # `resolve_exclude_globs` -> `normalize_globs`, so it is already
            # normalised. Re-normalising would be a call no test could reach and
            # no mutant could kill. That the normalisation really does happen
            # upstream is pinned behaviourally by
            # `test_a_messy_hand_typed_copy_of_the_default_still_gets_the_free_note`.
            free_to_drop = (g == AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB
                            and bool(summary.get("agent_glob_applied_by_default")))
            # 🟢 …AND THE REMEDY IS CONDITIONAL TOO. "--execute REFUSES while a
            # typed glob is in this state — pass --allow-unmatched-globs" was
            # printed unconditionally, including on runs where that flag was
            # ALREADY in force and `--execute` therefore does not refuse. Same
            # false-claim-about-this-very-run shape as the note above; fixed in
            # the same round rather than left as the next round's finding.
            #
            # 🔴 …AND IT IS THREE-WAY, matching `main()`'s three scopings rather
            # than two of them. Round 6 closed the `--allow-unmatched-globs`
            # exemption and left the NO-ROWS one open, so a scan that produced
            # no rows printed "--execute REFUSES while a typed glob is in this
            # state" on the stdout of a run that PROCEEDED (rc 0) while its own
            # stderr said "Not refusing." Order matches `main()`: no rows wins
            # over the override, because that is the branch `main()` takes first.
            if not summary.get("worktrees"):
                remedy = ("This scan produced NO rows at all, so the zero says nothing about "
                          "the glob and --execute does NOT refuse on it here — re-run against "
                          "a scope that actually holds worktrees before trusting this filter.")
            elif summary.get("allow_unmatched_globs"):
                remedy = ("--allow-unmatched-globs IS ALREADY IN FORCE, so --execute does NOT "
                          "refuse on this — it proceeds with this glob filtering nothing.")
            else:
                remedy = ("--execute REFUSES while a typed glob is in this state — pass "
                          "--allow-unmatched-globs if the glob is correct but out of this "
                          "scan's scope.")
            lines.append(
                f"🔴 --exclude-path {g!r} matched ZERO rows. A glob that matches nothing is "
                f"BYTE-IDENTICAL to passing no filter at all, and --confirm N cannot catch it "
                f"because N is derived from the same count. Check the spelling (and note that "
                f"`*` crosses `/`, a trailing `/` is stripped, and a literal path containing "
                f"`* ? [` needs glob.escape) before --execute. " + remedy
                + (f" (Note that {AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB!r} is applied by DEFAULT, so if this is a "
                   f"hand-typed copy of it you can simply drop it and lose nothing.)"
                   if free_to_drop else ""))
    # 🔴 OUTSIDE the `exclude_globs` block, because the flag can be in force with
    # NO globs of its own to print under: `--include-agent-worktrees
    # --allow-unmatched-globs` leaves the glob list empty, and the echo — which
    # `--help` and the header both promise happens "whenever it is in force" —
    # was silent. A description wider than its body is a claim the code does not
    # keep, so the body is widened rather than the claim narrowed.
    if summary.get("allow_unmatched_globs"):
        lines.append(
            "🔴 --allow-unmatched-globs IS IN FORCE: a --exclude-path glob matching ZERO "
            "rows only WARNS in this run, it does not refuse --execute. The zero still "
            "cannot tell a typo from a glob that is simply out of this scan's scope — "
            "you are asserting it is the latter.")
    if c[CANNOT_TELL]:
        lines.append(f"🔴 {c[CANNOT_TELL]} row(s) are cannot-tell — they are NOT dead and are "
                     "NOT removable. Read their verdict_reason before believing any count above.")
    if verbose:
        lines.append("")
        for r in sorted(rows, key=lambda x: (x["verdict"], x["path"])):
            lines.append(f"[{verdict_label(r)}] {r['path']}  branch={r['branch']}")
            lines.append(f"    {r['verdict_reason']}")
            for e in r["evidence"]:
                lines.append(f"      · {e}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


# ── execute ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def execute_removals(rows: "list[dict]", confirm: "int | None") -> int:
    """Remove ONLY `removable` rows, re-verifying each immediately beforehand.

    🔴 The re-verification is not belt-and-braces. The scan that produced these
    rows is a claim about the PAST; a concurrent session can have started
    editing in one of those worktrees since. The check therefore runs at the
    moment of the destructive step, not in the survey that motivated it.

    🔴 `git worktree remove` is called WITHOUT `--force`. Git refuses on a dirty
    or locked tree, which is an INDEPENDENT floor under our own dirty check —
    two mechanisms, so a bug in ours is not the only thing standing there.
    """
    targets = [r for r in rows if r.get("removable")]
    if confirm is None:
        _log(f"REFUSED: --execute requires --confirm N. This run would remove {len(targets)} "
             f"worktree(s); pass --confirm {len(targets)} if that is what you intend.")
        return RC_EXECUTE_REFUSED
    if confirm != len(targets):
        _log(f"REFUSED: --confirm {confirm} does not match the {len(targets)} row(s) this run "
             "would remove. The scope moved under you — re-read the report. Nothing removed.")
        return RC_EXECUTE_REFUSED

    removed = skipped = failed = 0
    for r in targets:
        path = Path(r["path"])
        repo = Path(r["repo"])
        if r.get("verdict") != DEAD:
            _log(f"SKIP {path}: verdict is {r.get('verdict')}, not {DEAD}")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        # Second, independent floor under the exclusion — same reasoning as the
        # verdict re-assert above: the executor does not trust the `removable`
        # flag it was handed.
        if r.get("excluded_by"):
            _log(f"SKIP {path}: excluded by path filter {r['excluded_by']!r}")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        live = [w for w in list_worktrees(repo) if Path(w["path"]) == path]
        if not live:
            _log(f"SKIP {path}: git no longer lists this worktree")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        if live[0]["locked"]:
            _log(f"SKIP {path}: locked since the scan")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        # 🔴 LAST, so it is the check immediately before the destructive step
        # rather than one buried in the survey that motivated it.
        dirty, count, _ = worktree_dirty(path)
        if dirty is not False:
            _log(f"SKIP {path}: re-check says dirty={dirty} ({count} path(s)) — "
                 "it changed since the scan, or was never knowable")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        res = _git(repo, "worktree", "remove", str(path))
        if res.returncode == 0:
            _log(f"REMOVED {path}")
            removed += 1
        else:
            _log(f"FAILED  {path}: rc={res.returncode} {res.stderr.strip()[:300]}")
            failed += 1
    _log(f"execute: removed={removed} skipped={skipped} failed={failed}")
    return RC_OK if failed == 0 else 1


# ── cli ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="worktree-prune",
        # 🔴 NO PREFIX ABBREVIATION. argparse's default (`allow_abbrev=True`)
        # accepts any unambiguous prefix, which quietly voids this tool's whole
        # design claim that "the dangerous action is the one the operator has to
        # type": MEASURED on the default parser, `--i` alone set
        # --include-agent-worktrees and `--all` set --allow-unmatched-globs. Both
        # are safety opt-outs, and neither was typed. Pinned by
        # `test_dangerous_flags_cannot_be_reached_by_abbreviation`.
        allow_abbrev=False,
        description="Classify git worktrees as dead / orphan / live / cannot-tell. "
                    "DRY-RUN BY DEFAULT — nothing is removed without --execute.")
    p.add_argument("--repo", action="append", default=[], metavar="DIR",
                   help="a repository to scan (repeatable)")
    p.add_argument("--scan-root", action="append", default=[], metavar="DIR",
                   help="discover main checkouts under DIR (repeatable)")
    p.add_argument("--repos-from", metavar="FILE",
                   help="file of repository paths, one per line; # comments allowed")
    p.add_argument("--scan-depth", type=int, default=2,
                   help="how deep --scan-root descends looking for checkouts (default 2)")
    p.add_argument("--exclude-path", action="append", default=[], metavar="GLOB",
                   help="spare any worktree whose ABSOLUTE path matches GLOB (repeatable). "
                        "fnmatch semantics and `*` CROSSES `/`, so '*/civitai/*' matches every "
                        "path with a civitai component. SUBTREE semantics: matching a "
                        "worktree's ANCESTOR excludes it too, so naming a directory covers "
                        "everything under it. A trailing '/' is stripped (tab-completion adds "
                        "one) and the symlink-resolved path is tested as well as the recorded "
                        "one. A literal path containing '* ? [' does NOT match itself — run it "
                        "through glob.escape first. An excluded row is still classified and "
                        "still printed (as `<verdict> (excluded)`), is NEVER removed, and is NOT "
                        "counted toward --confirm N. Every glob's match count is echoed in the "
                        "report, and --execute REFUSES while a glob you TYPED matches ZERO rows "
                        "(see --allow-unmatched-globs).")
    p.add_argument("--allow-unmatched-globs", action="store_true",
                   help="downgrade the zero-match refusal to a loud WARNING. A zero match cannot "
                        "distinguish a MISTYPED glob from a correct one that is simply out of "
                        "this scan's scope — a fleet-wide exclude list pointed at a single repo "
                        "matches nothing and is entirely right. Pass this to assert the second "
                        "reading. The glob is still named per-glob in the report, and the "
                        "override itself is echoed prominently there.")
    p.add_argument("--include-agent-worktrees", action="store_true",
                   help=f"🔴 DANGEROUS. Turn OFF the default exclusion of {AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB!r} "
                        "and treat worktrees under a `.claude/worktrees/` directory as ordinary "
                        "removal candidates. They belong to OTHER LIVE Claude sessions and "
                        "removing one destroys a working directory that is in use RIGHT NOW — on "
                        "the scan this default was measured from (2026-08-27), 137 of 236 `dead` rows "
                        "were such trees. The exclusion covers EVERY entry under that directory, not "
                        "just the `agent-` prefixed ones (2 of 246 such entries on this box have "
                        "no `agent-` prefix and are live sessions' trees). When this flag "
                        "actually turns the exclusion OFF the report says so, loudly. Re-type "
                        "the same glob as --exclude-path and the exclusion stays in force; the "
                        "report then prints a quiet `note:` saying so instead, because the loud "
                        "warning would be false.")
    p.add_argument("--jobs", type=int, default=8, help="parallel worktree probes (default 8)")
    p.add_argument("--no-gh", action="store_true",
                   help="do not consult gh. Every unlanded row then becomes cannot-tell, "
                        "because 'no PR found' and 'we could not ask' stop being distinguishable.")
    p.add_argument("--gh-cmd", default="gh", help="the gh executable to use (default: gh)")
    p.add_argument("--pr-limit", type=int, default=2000,
                   help="how many PRs to pull per repo before falling back to per-branch queries")
    p.add_argument("--format", choices=("text", "json"), default="text")
    p.add_argument("--out", metavar="FILE", help="write the machine-readable row list here")
    p.add_argument("--verbose", action="store_true", help="print every row with its evidence")
    p.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                   help="explicit no-op: dry-run is already the default")
    p.add_argument("--execute", action="store_true",
                   help="actually remove the `dead` rows. Requires --confirm N.")
    p.add_argument("--confirm", type=int, metavar="N",
                   help="the exact number of worktrees --execute may remove")
    return p


def collect_repos(args) -> "list[Path]":
    repos: list[Path] = [Path(r).expanduser() for r in args.repo]
    if args.repos_from:
        for line in Path(args.repos_from).expanduser().read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
            line = line.split("#", 1)[0].strip()
            if line:
                repos.append(Path(line).expanduser())
    if args.scan_root:
        repos.extend(discover_repos([Path(r) for r in args.scan_root], args.scan_depth))
    seen: dict[str, Path] = {}
    for r in repos:
        # Same best-effort resolve as everywhere else — an unresolvable path
        # de-duplicates by its literal spelling rather than taking the scan down.
        real = _resolve_or_none(r)
        seen.setdefault(str(real) if real is not None else str(r), r)
    return list(seen.values())


def main(argv: "list[str] | None" = None) -> int:
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    if args.dry_run and args.execute:
        _log("--dry-run and --execute are contradictory. Pick one.")
        return RC_USAGE
    if args.confirm is not None and not args.execute:
        _log("--confirm is only meaningful with --execute.")
        return RC_USAGE
    # 🔴 A blank --exclude-path is silently a no-op filter, which is the exact
    # class of failure this flag exists to prevent. Refuse it rather than drop it.
    # `normalize_glob` is THE spelling of "what this value means" — this check
    # and the resolver used to strip in different orders, and a glob with a
    # trailing space slipped through both.
    for raw in args.exclude_path:
        if not normalize_glob(raw):
            _log(f"--exclude-path was given an EMPTY glob ({raw!r}). An empty filter excludes "
                 "nothing and is indistinguishable from passing no filter at all. Remove the "
                 "flag or spell the glob.")
            return RC_USAGE

    repos = collect_repos(args)
    if not repos:
        _log("nothing to scan: pass --repo, --repos-from or --scan-root.")
        return RC_NOTHING_TO_SCAN

    exclude_globs = resolve_exclude_globs(args.exclude_path, args.include_agent_worktrees)
    typed_globs = normalize_globs(args.exclude_path)

    rows: list[dict] = []
    for repo in repos:
        rows.extend(scan_repo(repo, args.gh_cmd, not args.no_gh, args.pr_limit, args.jobs,
                              exclude_globs))

    summary = summarize(rows, exclude_globs, typed_globs,
                        agent_worktrees_included=args.include_agent_worktrees,
                        allow_unmatched_globs=args.allow_unmatched_globs)
    payload = {"summary": summary, "rows": rows}

    if args.out:
        Path(args.out).expanduser().write_text(
            json.dumps(payload, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")

    if args.format == "json":
        print(json.dumps(payload, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
    else:
        print(render_text(rows, summary, args.verbose))

    if not args.execute:
        _log("DRY RUN — nothing was removed. Pass --execute --confirm N to act.")
        return RC_OK

    # 🔴 A TYPED glob matching nothing is BYTE-IDENTICAL to no glob at all, so
    # the operator reads the full removable count as if their filter were in
    # force — and `--confirm N` is no help, because N comes from that same count.
    # So it REFUSES rather than warning.
    #
    # 🔴 THREE scopings, each closing a way this check went wrong:
    #   * TYPED globs only. The default agent glob is a constant that cannot be
    #     mistyped; refusing on it made the safety default block ITSELF on any
    #     repo with no `.claude/worktrees` directory, with "drop the safety" as
    #     the only remedy the message could offer. A permanently-red gate trains
    #     the click-through, and here the click-through IS the catastrophe.
    #   * Not when the scan produced NO ROWS. With zero rows nothing could have
    #     matched, so the zero is evidence about the SCAN and not about the glob
    #     — and this refusal broke the previously-working `--execute --confirm 0`
    #     no-op on an empty scope.
    #   * `--allow-unmatched-globs` downgrades it to a warning, because a zero
    #     match cannot tell a typo from a correct fleet-wide glob aimed at a
    #     narrow scan. The override is echoed in the report as well as here.
    dud = summary["exclude_globs_blocking_execute"]
    if dud and not rows:
        _log("note: " + ", ".join(repr(g) for g in dud) + " matched zero rows, but this scan "
             "produced NO rows at all — the zero says nothing about the glob. Not refusing.")
    elif dud and args.allow_unmatched_globs:
        _log("WARNING (--allow-unmatched-globs): " + ", ".join(repr(g) for g in dud) +
             f" matched ZERO of the {len(rows)} scanned row(s). Without that flag this run "
             "would have REFUSED. A glob that matches nothing is indistinguishable from no "
             "filter, and --confirm cannot catch that because it is derived from the same "
             "count — you are asserting the glob is correct but out of this scan's scope. "
             "Proceeding.")
    elif dud:
        # 🔴 "Drop it" is one of the three remedies, and for a hand-typed copy of
        # the default agent glob it is a FREE one — the constant is applied to
        # every run anyway. Saying so makes every remedy this message offers
        # visibly safe; without it "drop it" reads as "drop your protection",
        # which is the remedy phrasing that trained a click-through in round 2.
        # 🔴 …and ONLY while the constant is ACTUALLY applied. `--execute` with
        # `--include-agent-worktrees` does NOT get the constant appended
        # (`resolve_exclude_globs`), so the hand-typed copy is the only thing
        # sparing agent worktrees on that command line. Telling the operator it
        # is free to drop, in a refusal they must get past, advises the
        # DELETING-MORE direction — and the number behind it is 137 live
        # sessions' working directories. Gated on the FACT, not on the glob's
        # spelling, which is the same mistake the round-4 shout fix closed.
        #
        # 🟢 …and scoped PER-GLOB rather than used as a boolean: the sentence
        # above names EVERY dud, so an unqualified parenthetical reads as
        # "dropping any of them is free". At most one dud can equal the constant
        # (the typed list is normalised and deduped), but the message must say
        # which one, and must say the others are NOT free.
        #
        # 🟡 AND THE CONTRAST MUST NAME THE REAL DISTINCTION, WHICH IS
        # AUTO-RESTORED vs NOT — not "what is spared". Round 5 said "dropping
        # your hand-typed copy of it changes nothing about what is spared;
        # dropping '*/civitai/*' really would". Every glob named in this refusal
        # matched ZERO rows — that is what put it in `dud` — so `*/civitai/*`
        # spares nothing either and dropping it changes nothing about what is
        # spared in this scan. An operator reading that keeps an inert filter,
        # believes it is load-bearing, and reaches for --allow-unmatched-globs to
        # get past the refusal with a filter that filters nothing. What is
        # actually true: drop the constant and `resolve_exclude_globs` puts it
        # back; drop any other glob and it is gone from future runs too.
        #
        # 🔴 ONE TEMPLATE, BOTH BRANCHES. The solo and mixed wordings used to be
        # two independently-written sentences about the same fact — the shape
        # this whole round exists to remove — so `others` now only decides
        # whether the scoping clause and the contrast are appended.
        redundant = ([g for g in dud if g == AGENT_WORKTREE_GLOB]
                     if summary["agent_glob_applied_by_default"] else [])
        others = [g for g in dud if g not in redundant]
        if redundant:
            free_note = (", ".join(repr(g) for g in redundant)
                         + (" — and ONLY that one —" if others else "")
                         + " is applied by DEFAULT to every run, so dropping your hand-typed "
                           "copy of it leaves it in force: the tool puts it back. ")
            if others:
                free_note += ("Dropping " + ", ".join(repr(g) for g in others) + " really "
                              "removes it, from every future run too — though none of the "
                              "globs named in this refusal is sparing anything in THIS scan, "
                              "because every one of them matched zero rows. ")
        else:
            free_note = ""
        _log("REFUSED: " + ", ".join(repr(g) for g in dud) +
             f" matched ZERO of the {len(rows)} scanned row(s). A filter that matches nothing "
             "is indistinguishable from no filter, and --confirm cannot catch that because it "
             "is derived from the same count. Fix the glob (`*` crosses `/`; a trailing `/` is "
             "stripped; a literal path containing `* ? [` needs glob.escape), drop it, or pass "
             "--allow-unmatched-globs if it is correct but out of this scan's scope. "
             + free_note
             + "Nothing removed.")
        return RC_EXECUTE_REFUSED
    return execute_removals(rows, args.confirm)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
